## Proof

**Yes**, such an enumeration exists.

**Construction.** Fix $M = 1$ and let $I = [-1, 1]$. Partition $\mathbb{N}$ into square indices $\{m^2 : m \in \mathbb{N}\} = \{1, 4, 9, 16, \dots\}$ and non-square indices. The rationals in $I$ and the rationals outside $I$ are both countably infinite, as are the non-square and square indices respectively. Define the enumeration $\{r_n\}$ by:
- Assigning the rationals in $I$ bijectively to the non-square indices.
- Assigning the rationals outside $I$ bijectively to the square indices $n = m^2$.

This is a valid enumeration of all rationals.

**Key observations.** Let $U = \bigcup_{n=1}^\infty (r_n - 1/n,\; r_n + 1/n)$.

**(1) Non-square-index intervals lie in $[-2, 2]$.** For non-square $n$, we have $r_n \in [-1, 1]$, so $(r_n - 1/n,\; r_n + 1/n) \subset [-1-1, 1+1] = [-2, 2]$ (since $1/n \leq 1$). Thus
$$\bigcup_{\text{non-square } n} (r_n - 1/n,\; r_n + 1/n) \subset [-2, 2].$$

**(2) Square-index intervals have finite total measure.** The square-index intervals are $(r_{m^2} - 1/m^2,\; r_{m^2} + 1/m^2)$, each of length $2/m^2$. Their total measure satisfies:
$$\sum_{m=1}^{\infty} \frac{2}{m^2} = \frac{\pi^2}{3} < \infty.$$
Hence the set $S = \bigcup_{m=1}^\infty (r_{m^2} - 1/m^2,\; r_{m^2} + 1/m^2)$ has measure at most $\pi^2/3$.

**(3) The complement of $U$ is non-empty.** We have $U \subset [-2, 2] \cup S$. The complement $\mathbb{R} \setminus [-2, 2] = (-\infty, -2) \cup (2, \infty)$ has infinite measure. Since $S$ has measure at most $\pi^2/3 < \infty$, it cannot cover all of $\mathbb{R} \setminus [-2, 2]$. Therefore there exists a point $x \in \mathbb{R} \setminus ([-2, 2] \cup S)$.

Such an $x$ is not in $[-2,2]$, so it is not in any non-square-index interval (by (1)), and it is not in $S$, so it is not in any square-index interval (by (2)). Hence $x \notin U$, i.e., $x \in \mathbb{R} \setminus U$.

In fact, $\mathbb{R} \setminus U$ has infinite measure, since $\mathbb{R} \setminus [-2,2]$ has infinite measure and $S$ covers only finitely much of it.

**Conclusion.** The enumeration constructed above satisfies $\mathbb{R} \setminus \bigcup_{n=1}^\infty (r_n - 1/n,\; r_n + 1/n) \neq \emptyset$. $\blacksquare$

